WEBVTT

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:04.010 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
At a grocery store, a shopper
picks one item from a shelf.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.520 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Amazon's Just Walk Out uses
cameras and shelf sensors

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.580 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
to keep track of what people take or put back.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.990 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Retail can also be studied in a virtual store.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:17.630 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Here a researcher moves through
the Sari Sandbox simulation.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:27.160 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Virtual try-on lets a shopper preview an
item, such as glasses, on their own image.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:32.760 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A Transferable Variant Signal That
Grocery Retrieval Cannot Reach.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:39.000 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Emmanuel G. Maminta and Rowel O. Atienza,

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.300 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Artificial Intelligence Graduate Program,
University of the Philippines Diliman.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.360 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
An oral presentation at the Asian
Conference on Computer Vision in Osaka.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:56.880 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Our study asks how a checkout system
identifies the exact product for a receipt.

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:01.330 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A model compares a camera photo
with written catalog descriptions.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:03.770 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
This is called multimodal product retrieval.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:08.980 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The model turns the photo and each
description into lists of numbers.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:12.980 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
It scores each pair by how
closely their directions line up.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:21.190 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
This can is Campbell's Creamy Tomato.
The model ranks Chicken Noodle first.

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:24.350 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
It found the brand, but missed the flavor.

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:34.830 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
In earlier work, we tested 190 image
and text encoders on GroceryVision.

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:40.480 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For SigLIP2 at 384 pixels, the correct
product appeared in the first five choices

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.440 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
17.5 percentage points more
often than it ranked first.

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:49.010 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A shortlist can contain the right answer
while the receipt still lists the wrong item.

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:52.290 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
When the model chooses the wrong flavor,

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:55.970 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
do its image measurements still
tell the two flavors apart?

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:57.920 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
And can the differences learned

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.500 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
from some brands help identify
products from a new brand?

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:08.570 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
First, we freeze the image encoder.
Its measurements of a photo stay fixed.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:12.520 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We train a small classifier,
called a linear probe,

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:17.460 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
to distinguish varieties within one
brand. Here the varieties are almonds.

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:24.550 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
On separate photos, SigLIP2 features
give 97.1 percent balanced accuracy.

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:27.310 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Each variety counts equally within its brand.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:35.240 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
So the image features retain distinctions
that the comparison with text can miss.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:42.280 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We make a second version of the
training set. The photos stay in place.

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:47.810 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Shuffle the variety names within the
brand. Each name still appears twice here.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:52.660 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The test photo keeps its correct
label. We will fit the directions again

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:56.540 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
using the shuffled training
groups, then compare the results.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:01.560 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Start with the correct names.
Imagine one dot for each photo,

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.540 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
placed according to the
features of that image.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:09.590 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Average the dots for each product. Then find
the center of the products in each brand.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.840 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
An arrow from that center to a product shows
how the variety differs from its brand.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:25.450 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Each arrow becomes a row of a table. Each
column corresponds to one image feature.

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:31.070 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Imagine turning a ruler through
the center of the arrows.

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:34.570 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Drop each tip straight down onto the ruler.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:38.540 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Some angles spread the marks
apart more than others.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.660 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Keep the direction with the greatest spread.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:43.890 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The next direction captures
the most remaining variation

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.030 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
at a right angle to the first.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:51.470 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Singular value decomposition finds
these directions in the full table.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:58.640 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The first matrix is called U. Each column
gives the weights for one direction.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:03.480 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A plus or minus sign tells
which way each variety lies.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:06.950 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Sigma gives the strength of that direction.

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:11.570 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Each row of V transpose gives the
direction in the original image features.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:17.820 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We keep the strongest 64 directions.
Together they form the variant subspace.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:23.260 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We learned those directions from the
training brands. Starbucks was absent.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:28.490 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We project the photo and each
description onto those directions.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:32.610 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
That means measuring both
with the same rulers.

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:37.110 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We draw the arrows the same
length, then compare their angles.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.880 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Ten test photos show Sumatra coffee.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:44.320 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Their average image vector scores
highest against the Sumatra description.

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:48.030 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Using the directions
fitted with shuffled names,

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.890 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
the same average ranks Pike Place first.

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:54.550 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
We repeat the experiment ten times.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:59.610 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Each time we fit the directions on
one set of brands and test on others.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:05.040 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
For SigLIP2, correct labels improve the
first choice by 13.6 percentage points

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.580 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
over shuffled labels across
all 409 descriptions.

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:14.510 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
When every candidate belongs to the
correct brand, the gain is 3.9 points.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.700 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
So most of the gain comes from
fewer matches to the wrong brand.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:24.520 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The full, original embedding still
gives the highest retrieval accuracy.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:30.600 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
The shopper chose a particular flavor.
The receipt needs to name that variety.

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:36.150 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
Our probe showed that image
features could tell varieties apart.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:41.570 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
On new brands, the learned directions
mostly corrected brand mistakes.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:47.480 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
A useful visual representation preserves
the difference between two products.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:53.260 line:74% align:center position:50% size:90%
To identify the exact item, the comparison
with text must use that difference too.
